# A menu as a piece of writing with a layout: headings that group, one dish and its price to a
# line, and a describing word that sells the dish.
lesson writing-a-cafe-menu v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=6 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A cafe menu"
  goal "Write a menu with headings, prices and one describing word for each dish, laid out the way a real menu is."

  look {
    scene 30x13 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=3 align=start {
        sign board lines=["The Harbour Cafe", "Drinks", "Hot tea $2.00", "Cakes", "Fresh scone $3.50"] kind=board color=mint
        pricetag tag now=350 was=0
      }
    }
    say """
      Sam is writing the menu for the cafe by the post office. A menu is read fast, by a hungry
      customer: headings say where to look, each dish sits on its own line with its price at the
      end, and one describing word before the dish makes them want it. Hot tea, fresh scone. The
      price is written the way money is written: the sign, the dollars, a point and two figures.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.price-wrong k=0 r=0
    }
    practice writing.which-heading count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice writing.dishes-under count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=3 hard-seed=2
    practice writing.price-wrong count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
    practice writing.sells-the-dish count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice writing.menu-count count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice writing.commas-in-a-list count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=2
    level hard {
      show writing.sells-the-dish k=1 p=2 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show writing.menu-story h=3 d=4 w=0 easy-h=2 easy-d=3 hard-h=4 hard-d=5 hard-w=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.easier-menu k=0 r=1 hard-k=1 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.own-menu k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "A menu has headings, one dish and its price to a line, and a word that sells each dish."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a menu is writing with a layout: headings that group the dishes, one dish
    and its price to a line, prices written as money is, and a describing word chosen to sell
    rather than only to be true. The look is the cafe board and a price tag. The way in finds
    the heading among the lines. The core sorts dishes under headings, fixes a price written
    wrongly, picks the word that sells a dish, reads a board for a count and a total, and
    counts the commas in a list of dishes. The wrong idea to watch for is that any true word
    will do before a dish (cold soup is true of some soup); ask whether the word would make the
    child order it. The story counts a menu by its headings and dishes, the two-star question
    asks which of two boards is easier to read and why, and the three-star question is the
    child's own menu on the frame, which you mark: a dish under each heading, its price written
    properly, a selling word before it. Menus at home or in the town are worth reading together
    for their headings and their words.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the price three dollars fifty is worked once in the teacher's pen, the heading is among the drinks, the dish in the wrong column is a plain one, the price to fix is three dollars fifty again with its options shuffled, the word to choose is for the soup, the board is read for a count, the commas are in a short list, the story has two headings of three or four dishes, the two-star's laid-out menu is A, and the child's menu has one dish a heading."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the heading is among the puddings or the mains, the dishes in the wrong column are the pie under the puddings, which could pass, and a soup or a cake, the price to fix has five cents or eighty, the word to choose is for the chips with a second, the cake's, in the core, the board is read for a total, the commas are in a longer list, the story has four headings of four or five dishes and asks for every line, the two-star's laid-out menu is B, and the child's menu has two dishes a heading with no word repeated."
  }
}
